    "result_text": "Tests are green. \n\n## Summary\n\nI agree with the finding \u2014 the `@`-splitting was re-implemented at both call sites, the exact split-brain `DisposableDomains` was introduced to prevent. Fixed by pushing the email-level entry points into the util so domain parsing lives once, next to the data it feeds:\n\n- **`DisposableDomains`** \u2014 added public `isDisposableEmail(String email)` and `isDisposableAdjacentEmail(String email)`, backed by a single private `domainOf(email)` helper that owns the `@`-extraction (null / no-domain \u2192 treated as not disposable).\n- **`EmailDomainValidator.isValid`** \u2014 now just `return !DisposableDomains.isDisposableEmail(email);`. Behaviour is identical: null emails and domainless values yield `false` from the util, so the validator still accepts them and defers to the `@Email` constraint.\n- **`OwnerMapper`** \u2014 removed the private `isDisposableAdjacentEmail(Owner)` string-parsing helper; `formatRiskFlag` now calls `DisposableDomains.isDisposableAdjacentEmail(owner.getEmail())` directly, so the mapper interface no longer embeds raw domain parsing.\n\nAcceptance behaviour is unchanged (`riskFlag` = possible-duplicate \u2228 disposable-adjacent email \u2228 over soft capacity), and the full `mvn test` suite passes (`BUILD SUCCESS`).",
