    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe centralization is the right instinct \u2014 `DisposableDomains` as a single home for the blocklist, reused by both the validator and the mapper, and `formatRiskFlag` reusing the existing `formatPossibleDuplicate`/`formatCapacityWarning` helpers is clean and idiomatic. One structural issue, though:\n\n**Email-domain extraction is duplicated across the two call sites.** The logic that pulls the domain out of an email address \u2014\n\n```java\nint at = email.lastIndexOf('@');\nif (at < 0 || at == email.length() - 1) { ... }\nemail.substring(at + 1)\n```\n\n\u2014 now appears verbatim in both `EmailDomainValidator.isValid` (lines 21\u201325) and `OwnerMapper.isDisposableAdjacentEmail` (lines 74\u201378). This is the exact split-brain the new class was introduced to prevent: `DisposableDomains`' own javadoc says the knowledge is \"kept in one place so the create-time validation and the owner risk flag agree on the same list,\" yet how each site derives the *domain* from an email is re-implemented independently. If that parsing ever needs to change (trimming, odd edge cases), the two can silently diverge.\n\nConcrete fix: push the email-level entry points into `DisposableDomains` so the `@`-splitting lives once, next to the data it feeds:\n\n```java\npublic static boolean isDisposableEmail(String email) { ... }\npublic static boolean isDisposableAdjacentEmail(String email) { ... }\n```\n\nThen `EmailDomainValidator` calls `isDisposableEmail(email)` and `OwnerMapper.formatRiskFlag` calls `DisposableDomains.isDisposableAdjacentEmail(owner.getEmail())` directly. That also removes `OwnerMapper`'s private `isDisposableAdjacentEmail(Owner)` helper, which is the one method in the file doing raw string-parsing rather than being a MapStruct `format*`/`@Named` mapping \u2014 domain-parsing logic sits more naturally in the util than embedded in the mapper interface.",
